DECISION NEEDED (maintainer) A — integer-like object keys. JS orders own keys as array-index keys ascending first, then string keys by insertion (OrdinaryOwnPropertyKeys); ADR 0011 only says insertion order (Hash::Ordered) for literals/engine-built objects and sorted for plain caller hashes. Oracle: groupby over years gives 9|10|2018|2019|b|a. Options: (1) amend ADR 0011 — the one ordering seam applies the JS rule to literal and engine-built dicts, and plain caller hashes sort array-index keys numerically first, then the rest by key; (2) amend for literal/engine-built only, plain hashes stay purely sorted; (3) keep pure insertion order and record the integer-key divergence. Recommendation: (1) — matches JS wherever the information exists and narrows the plain-hash divergence. Until decided: groupby/dump/list/dictsort/urlencode ported with insertion order, the 2 groupby integer-key assertions in t/upstream/filters.t stay TODO, integer-key corpus cases go in as TODO.

DECISION NEEDED (maintainer) B — Array.prototype.sort with an inconsistent comparator (sort/dictsort over mixed kinds, e.g. [1,'a',true,null]). ES2019 sort is stable, so every consistent comparator gives the same result in a stable Perl sort; only inconsistent comparators expose V8's algorithm (oracle: sort-mixed ',1,true,2,9,10,a'). Options: (1) port V8's Array.prototype.sort (TimSort with binary insertion for short runs) as one Lib seam, pinned to the oracle's Node, oracle corpus for mixed arrays; (2) use Perl's stable sort and record a divergence limited to inconsistent comparators. Recommendation: (1) — fits the maintainer's fidelity stance in ADR 0007/0012 ('Aufwand ist immer ok'), but it ports V8 code rather than Nunjucks code, so it is the maintainer's call. Until decided: sort/dictsort ported with stable Perl sort for consistent comparators; mixed-kind inputs go in as TODO corpus cases.

DECISION NEEDED (maintainer) C — JS values with no Perl form, used by the upstream 'length' tests: new String('...') (wrapper object) and an Array with user-defined properties (Map/Set and the custom-toString object belong to k44). Options: (1) one ADR recording them as inexpressible-input divergences, the tests stay skipped citing it (could cover k44's Map/Set too); (2) Perl shims (a Runtime String-wrapper class, an array-with-properties object) that length & co. recognise. Recommendation: (1) — no Perl caller produces these values naturally, and shims would be API surface nobody uses. Until decided: the 4 length tests stay skipped (as committed by the test-writer).

ORCHESTRATOR CALL (not a maintainer decision, overrule if wrong): builtins that read this.env (groupby/sort: opts.throwOnUndefined; select/reject: getTest) get the Environment by closure — Filters exports are built per Environment at registration — instead of a this-binding. Reason: ADR 0009 calls Context-as-this engine-internal plumbing and gives every sync filter (@args) only; a closure keeps that call shape and is unobservable from templates. Also: globals stay with k18 for this run (the run's instruction names filters only); the k17 body and ADR 0015 say globals at k17 — conflict flagged, ADR 0015 prose to be aligned.

CORRECTION to decision A (after the k34 fidelity audit): ADR 0011 already decides literal and engine-built dicts are 1:1 with JS, so JS array-index-first key order there is a bug in the mechanism, not an open mapping — filed as k51 (bug, high). The only open maintainer question left from A is k51's last paragraph: should plain caller hashes (sorted per ADR 0011) also sort array-index keys numerically first? Recommendation unchanged: yes. Decision C is folded into k44 (inexpressible JS inputs).

